  "worked_solution": "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **population** $P$ of mosquitos **increases** with time. The **rate** of change of the population of mosquitos is **proportional** to the **square of the population**.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *population* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *population squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}\u221d{P}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\u221d$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the population *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k{P}^{2}.\n$$",
